Negative control — the manager's CREATE limb (AC-24, parity row W8/L3).

Amputates the `add` service in `useClientAddressManagerServices` — the limb
`processing.adding` invokes when the `isNew` guard passes — so a save from a
`.fresh()` editor can never reach `service.ensure`, and therefore never reaches
the wire as a POST.

Should flip RED — __tests__/client-address.manager-save.int.test.ts:
  - "AC-24 posts the model I filled in, exactly once"
                                (no POST is captured; the save rejects)
  - "AC-24 adopts the created address's id, so the editor stops being new"
                                (nothing is created, so no id is adopted)

Why this is a SEPARATE control from `manager-amputation`. Create and update are
two distinct limbs of the same services adapter: `add` routes to
`service.ensure` (find-or-create -> POST) and `update` routes to the diffing
`update` (-> PUT). Amputating one leaves the other untouched, so a single
mutant covering both would not localise the failure — and the update-only
mutant demonstrably left BOTH AC-24 tests green. One mutant per capability:
`manager-amputation` declares AC-23 (the update/PUT limb), this one declares
AC-24 (the create/POST limb).

Note the scope this does NOT touch: `service.ensure` itself is unmutated, so
the collection's own find-or-create (AC-35, `unified` / `client-company`
callers) stays green. Only the editor's create path is amputated.

Measured under this mutant: exactly the two AC-24 tests above flip RED. The
suite's one standing failure (AC-20 / finding F6, escalated per
`review-notes.md` §7.6) is unrelated and red with or without it.

